EXT. FROZEN LAKE - NIGHT

Starlight on black ice. A hundred yards of nothing in every direction. Wind drags snow in low serpents across the surface.

A WOMAN (30s, hooded parka, ice axe in one fist, a rucksack on her back) crosses the lake, stepping with care, testing each footfall. Her breath fogs the air.

Fifty yards behind her, CALEB (30s, bare-headed, no gloves, a blue lip) staggers after, one arm hugging his chest.

CALEB
Wait!

She stops. Turns. Doesn't answer.

CALEB (CONT'D)
Please. I can't feel my feet.

WOMAN
Then walk faster.

He stumbles closer. She plants the ice axe and waits, wary.

CALEB
You're the first person I've seen in two days.

WOMAN
You're lucky it was me.

CALEB
Why?

WOMAN
The last one I saw tried to take my pack.

Caleb stops six feet away. He raises both hands, palms open, shaking.

CALEB
I'm not going to rob you. I'm going to die if you don't help me.

WOMAN
That's two different problems.

CALEB
Do you have anything to drink?

She hesitates. Then unslings her pack, pulls out a flask, and tosses it across the gap. He catches it, fumbles, drops it, and picks it up again in clumsy fingers. He drinks. Coughs. Drinks.

CALEB (CONT'D)
God. What is that?

WOMAN
Schnapps.

CALEB
It's wonderful.

WOMAN
It's terrible. But it's warm.

He lowers the flask, wipes his mouth on his sleeve. Hands it back.

CALEB
My name's Caleb. Caleb Hurley.

WOMAN
I didn't ask.

CALEB
I'm telling you anyway. In case.

WOMAN
In case of what?

CALEB
In case somebody finds me.

She studies him a moment. The wind bites. Somewhere out on the ice, a deep crack booms, and both of them flinch. The surface shifts beneath their feet.

WOMAN
(sharp)
Don't move.

CALEB
I'm not.

WOMAN
Spread your weight. Lie flat.

He drops to his belly, then spreads his arms. She does the same, ten feet away, sliding the axe out in front of her. The crack booms again, longer. Water seeps up along a seam between them.

CALEB
Is it going?

WOMAN
It might.

CALEB
Say something.

WOMAN
What?

CALEB
Anything. Your name.

A pause. The ice groans.

INES
(finally)
Ines.

CALEB
Ines. That's pretty.

INES
Shut up and crawl.

They begin to inch toward the far shore, side by side, hauling themselves along on their elbows. The seam spreads behind them, black water racing across the white. Ines glances at it and doesn't stop. Caleb laughs, a cracked giggle born of terror.

CALEB
Ines! We're going to make it!

INES
Don't say that.

They reach the shallows. The ice thickens. She hauls him up by the collar, and they stand, panting, on solid ground at the edge of a pine forest. Behind them, with a sound like a slow gunshot, the whole sheet they crossed splits and sinks.

Caleb stares. Ines hands him the flask again. He takes it, drinks, and passes it back. Neither speaks. They stand there, steaming, listening to the lake fall quiet.
